course: english
lesson: 13
title: Words to know, Years 3–4 (the statutory list, by idea)
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In England the National Curriculum gives a list of about 100 words that children in Years 3 and 4 (ages 7 to 9) should learn to spell: accident, actually, address, answer, believe, business, February, grammar, height, island, library, possession, separate and many more. They are not random hard words; most are words children need in their own writing all the time.
explain: A long list is easier in groups by meaning: time words (century, February, history), place words (address, island, position), thinking words (believe, consider, imagine). And every list word has a tricky bit; find it and underline it. In «guard» and «guide» it is the «gu»; in «fruit» the «ui»; in «heard» the «ear».
explain: Then give the tricky bit a story. There is «a rat» in «sep-a-rat-e». You should never be-LIE-ve a LIE. «Business» is busy + ness, so busy's y turned into i. «Grammar» ends in -ar, not -er. «Possession» has two pairs of s's, like two pairs of shoes. «Height» shares e-i-g-h with «weight», though it sounds like /ite/.
explain: Say words slowly when you spell them. Many people say «libry» and «Febuary», dropping a part in fast speech, but the spelling keeps every part: li-brar-y, Feb-ru-ar-y. And the real test of a word is using it in your own sentence: «Although it rained, we still played» shows you can spell «although» and also know what it means.
fact: The National Curriculum in England lists about 100 words for Years 3–4 that children should learn to spell.
fact: Most words on the list are common in everyday writing, not rare hard words.
fact: Grouping list words by meaning (time, place, thinking) and learning a few each week, not all in one night, makes them easier to learn.
fact: Most list words have one tricky part: «gu» in guard and guide (the u is written but not said), «ui» in fruit, «ear» in heard.
fact: «Believe» has «lie» inside it: never believe a lie.
fact: «Separate» has «a rat» in it: sep-a-rat-e.
fact: «Business» is busy + ness, with the y changed to i.
fact: «Grammar» ends in -ar, not -er.
fact: «Height» is spelled h-e-i-g-h-t, like «weight», though it sounds like /ite/.
fact: «Library» has two r's: li-brar-y, though many people say «libry».
fact: «Possession» has four s's: po-ss-e-ss-ion.
fact: Using a list word in your own sentence checks its meaning as well as its spelling.
fact: Slang in this lesson: «peak» means bad luck or annoying (London); «the craic» is Irish for fun, and «that's the craic» can mean «that's how it is»; «sus» means suspicious; «no cap» means «no lie».
exercise: Mum moans there are «100 of these». About how many words are on the Years 3–4 list? | About 100 | Just about 10 | About 1,000 | Exactly 26
exercise: Why are these words on the list? | Children need them often in their own writing | They are the longest words in English | They are only used in tests | They were picked at random
exercise: Bella sorts words into time words and place words. Which is a time word? | century | address | island | position
exercise: Jacob writes «beleive». Which trick helps? | Never believe a lie | i comes before e after l | Think of a bee leaving | Believe has a bee buzzing at the very start
exercise: Anika writes «seperate». What is hidden in the right spelling? | a rat | a pear | a pet | an ape
exercise: Lily asks why «business» is so weird. Where does it come from? | busy + ness | bus + iness, like a bus | buzz + ness, like a bee | busi, an old word
exercise: Fynn says «grammar» sounds like «gram-er». How does it end? | -ar | -er | -or | -our
exercise: Charlie says «guard» and «guide» are boring. What makes them tricky? | A u after the g that you write but don't say | The silent d at the end | They have no vowels | Every letter is said aloud
exercise: Aoife in Dublin is surprised by the gh in «height». Which word shares its spelling? | weight | night | white | flight, with igh
exercise: Leo says «libry». How many r's are there in «library»? | 2 | 1 | 3 | 0
exercise: How many s's are in «possession»? | 4 | 2 | 3 | 5
exercise: Why does the teacher ask for «although» in a sentence? | It checks meaning as well as spelling | It is quicker than a spelling test | Sentences help handwriting only | Teachers love long homework
exercise: Which spelling of the second month is right? | February | Febuary | Feburary | Februray
exercise: «I ___ a noise.» Which spelling of the past of «hear» is right? | heard | herd (for listened) | haerd | hured
exercise: What does «no cap» mean? | No lie, for real | No hats allowed inside | No capital letters | There is no limit
exercise: What does «peak» mean in London slang? | Bad luck, annoying | Brilliant | The top of a hill | Very quiet
test: «hundred words, innit, I'll learn 'em in alphabetical order, one night». What is better? | A few each week, in groups by meaning | All 100 in one night, A to Z | Only the short ones | None, you'll pick them up from telly
test: «seperate looks right to me, bro». Which trick fixes it? | There's «a rat» in separate | Separate rhymes with «per» | Separate has «a pet» in the middle of it | Write it how it sounds
test: «beleive, that's peak, I always get it wrong». What should Jacob look for? | The hidden word «lie» in «believe» | The hidden word «eve» in «believe» | The hidden word «bee» in «believe» | The hidden word «leaf» in «believe»
test: What would happen if Leo always spelled words the way he says them fast? | He'd drop parts, like «libry» for «library» | His spelling would always be right | His handwriting would get neater and faster too | Nothing, fast speech has every part
test: «hight, h-i-g-h-t, sorted». What did Aoife's friend miss? | The e: height is h-e-i-g-h-t | The silent k: kheight | A double t at the end | Nothing, «hight» is right
test: «business is just bizz-ness, so b-i-z-n-e-s-s». Put him right. | It's busy + ness: b-u-s-i-n-e-s-s | He's right, it is spelled bizness | It's bus + iness: b-u-s-i-n-e-s-s-e | It's b-u-s-y-n-e-s-s
test: «grammer is sus, it should end in -er like hammer». Is Fynn right? | No, «grammar» ends in -ar | Yes, change it to «grammer» | Yes, both spellings are correct | No, it ends in -our, like colour
test: «learn possession tonight», no cap, it's got loads of s's. How many s's, and how are they grouped? | Four: two pairs, ss and ss | Three: s, ss | Two: one pair | Five: sss at the start and ss at the end
test: A cartoon kid says «I can spell «although» so I know it». What's the better test? | Use it in a sentence of your own | Spell it backwards | Spell it ten times fast | Find it in a dictionary
test: ★★★ Which list has every word spelled correctly? | separate, believe, library, height | seperate, believe, library, height | separate, beleive, libary, height | separate, believe, library, hieght
test: ★★★ «Busy» becomes «business». Which other word changes its y to i in the same way? | happy → happiness | play → playful | enjoy → enjoyment | day → days
test: ★★★ How many of these list words have a «gu» where the u is not said: guard, guide, guess, fruit? | 3 | 4 | 2 | 1
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the statutory spelling list for Years 3 and 4. | It comprises approximately one hundred words in frequent use in pupils' writing | It comprises approximately one thousand words of exceptional rarity | It comprises words selected at random each year by the examination boards for the purpose of testing | It comprises only words of more than four syllables
exam: Ascertain the correct spelling of the word signifying «to set or keep apart». | separate | seperate | seperete | separete
exam: Identify the principal derivation of the word «business». | The adjective «busy» with the suffix -ness | The noun «bus» with the suffix -iness | The verb «buzz» with the suffix -ness | A word borrowed from Latin
exam: Which of the following methods most accurately verifies that a pupil both spells and comprehends a word from the list? | The composition by the pupil of a sentence employing the word | The recitation of the word letter by letter at speed | The copying of the word on ten successive occasions without reference to its meaning | The location of the word in an index
